<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Senzangakhona</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1762–<a href="/years/1816">1816</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Senzangakhona kaJama</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Senzangakhona was chief of the small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a> in the late 18th and early 19th centuries. He was the father of three Zulu kings, Shaka, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpande">Mpande</a>. Shaka was born of his union with Nandi and grew up in exile, and after Senzangakhona's death in 1816 he seized the chiefdom with Mthethwa support.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Senzangakhona was the son of the chief Jama kaNdaba and of Mthaniya Sibiya, and he succeeded on his father's death. His name is derived from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> word meaning he who acts with a good reason. In his time the Zulus were a small clan within the Mthethwa confederation ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingiswayo">Dingiswayo</a>. Ritual circumcision, though still practised, was slowly dying out among them, and neither Senzangakhona nor Shaka was circumcised.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He married at least sixteen women and had fourteen known sons; his daughters were not recorded. Shaka, his first son, was born to Nandi kaBhebhe of the eLangeni and is said to have been conceived during ukuhlobonga, a practice allowed to unmarried couples that excluded penetration. Nandi and Shaka were at first accepted into his kraal, where she was treated as a lesser wife and also bore him a daughter, Nomcuba. Senzangakhona would not acknowledge her as his chief consort, so Shaka was not the heir, and although the Great Wife, Mkabi, treated Nandi well, the relationship broke down and mother and son had to leave.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His official heir was his son Sigujana, who took over on his death in 1816. Sigujana's rule was short: Shaka, helped by his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingiswayo">Dingiswayo</a> and his maternal half-brother Ngwadi, had him assassinated and then raised the chieftaincy into a kingdom.</p><p>Two more of Senzangakhona's sons became king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a>, son of his sixth wife Mpikase kaMlilela Ngobese, killed Shaka in 1828 at present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dukuza">Stanger</a>, officially KwaDukuza, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>. Mpande, son of his ninth wife Songiya kaNgotsha Hlabisa, overthrew Dingane in 1840 and was the only one of his sons to give him grandchildren; Mpande's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo kaMpande</a> ruled before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> territory. Senzangakhona has been played by Conrad Magwaza in the television miniseries Shaka Zulu and by Senzo Radebe in Shaka Ilembe.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Senzangakhona” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shaka, a son of the Zulu chief <b>Senzangakhona</b> born around 1787, grew up in exile and rose as a warrior in Dingiswayo’s army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Shaka, spurned as an illegitimate son of King <b>Senzangakhona</b>, served as a warrior under Dingiswayo of the Mthethwa, who helped him become king of the Zulu; after Dingiswayo’s death around 1816 he led the whole Mthethwa alliance, defeated the Ndwandwe at the Mhlatuze River in 1820 and by…</blockquote><a href="/societies/zulu-kingdom">Social Hierarchy of the Zulu Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
